  "name": "Isla del Bluencanto (Puerto Rico)",
  "kind": "special",
  "confidence": "low",
  "drawnOn": "A320",
  "from": "2025-10",
  "commemorates": "Puerto Rico: JetBlue's service to the island.",
  "description": "Fuselage: JetBlue blue, plain ahead of the wing; from the wing aft it's covered in tropical art on the blue: orange flowers, pink-red flowers, a fan of yellow banana leaves, big orange fruits, a cluster of red berries, pale-green leaves, and light-blue and navy stripes round the tail cone. 'jetBlue' in white over the forward windows; '¡SOMOS BORICUA!' large in white on the belly. Tail: a jíbaro (a Puerto Rican countryman in a yellow straw pava hat and a light-blue shirt) among teal palm fronds on the blue fin. Engines blue. Designed by Puerto Rican artist Juan Gutiérrez Rovira (The Stencil Network), chosen by public vote.",
  "sources": [
    {
      "url": "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20251002169259/en/",
      "title": "JetBlue: Isla del Bluencanto release (Business Wire, 2025-10-02)",
      "accessed": "2026-11-22",
      "note": "A320 N657JB 'Isla del Bluencanto', unveiled at San Juan in October 2025: designed by Puerto Rican artist Juan Gutiérrez Rovira (The Stencil Network), chosen by public vote; 'Somos Boricua' on the belly. JetBlue's second Puerto Rico livery after Bluericua (2018)."
    },
    {
      "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N657JB_FLL_17.11.25_(55595075900).jpg",
      "title": "N657JB FLL 17.11.25 (55595075900).jpg (CC BY-SA 4.0)",
      "accessed": "2026-11-22",
      "note": "N657JB left side from below on approach at Fort Lauderdale (2025-11-17 by its title; Commons' metadata says 2026-10-17): fitted to shapes/A320.json (doors, nose, tail, fin tip; within 6 px, 0.15 m, camera 17 degrees below). The art's big colour masses, the fin's figure and the title read off it."
    }
